					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1267048&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607028163" alt="Ihp" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1267048)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;" >Ihp</a></span> <span class="odate time_1396845574 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Apr 2014 04:39</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2007444">
									

<p>So, it's a mind virus caused by something like Cthulhu that causes xenophobia, or, as the article so eloquently puts it:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You think we all got infected by some &quot;crazy mutant virus&quot; that's turning us all into racist zombies attacking and killing each other.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While it's a unique concept&#8230; I'm not too sure I like it. To me, it's saying 'racism and genocide aren't because of mob mentality, ignorance and charismatic yet horrible people, it's the fault of a mind bug that makes you act horrible.' Don't get me wrong, I'm all for demonizing ignorance, but&#8230; bleh. I'm probably just reading too much into it, but SCPs that take blame off of the human condition just irk me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>although due to the highly charismatic nature of the affected individuals</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does one measure charisma? This isn't D&amp;D, so I don't think that charisma can be put into a quantitative form, unless this is D&amp;D with Order of the Stick rules.</p>
<p>Also, the article has a rather blatant contradiction:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After 30-36 hours, all major alterations of the affected individual's brain cease.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This implies it can be stopped with enough time. If that's the case, why hasn't it worn off in the original town? The containment procedures essentially say this is the case, so why bother putting in something that says it can wear off if it doesn't wear off?</p>
<p>The concept and writing are good, and the interview is spot-on, so this at least saves it from a downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
