														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2007893">
									

<p>Much improved from the early draft - same basic idea, but much better writing.</p>
<p>I find myself asking though, what exactly <em>is</em> the effect of this Cthulu-like being? Does it take ordinary morons with their vague and confused prejudices and turn them into &quot;Davids&quot; - David Icke, David Duke, David Emory, or similar obsessed conspiracy theorists? Not really - they're crazy but focused and consistent, which the interview subject isn't.</p>
<p>Does it turn them into Fred Phelps - a man whose entire consciousness was warped around a single hatred, changing everything else to fit? Or does it turn them into Jesse Ventura or Alex Jones, men who never met a conspiracy theory they didn't like? Again, not really.</p>
<p>There's no breakdown of social order, or change in power structure. The people aren't deranged, or fanatical, just&#8230;hicks. Creationists, tea partiers, congregations of tiny evangelical chirches. It's like Stepford transposed to Iowa.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The interpretation of fictional events as real memories</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do we <em>have</em> a word for this? It's not &quot;confabulation&quot;, but we certainly <em>should</em> have a term for it.</p>

